## 6. Physical execution: pipelines + morsel-driven parallelism

Kùzu uses **morsel-driven parallelism** and explicitly breaks physical plans into pipelines.

* Internal docs: the **processor** contains a task scheduler that breaks a physical plan into multiple pipelines for morsel-driven parallelism. ([Kuzu][4])
* CIDR paper: parallel tasks run copies of the same pipeline and coordinate to fetch morsels of node IDs/properties to scan. ([CIDRDB][2])

Conceptually:

```mermaid
flowchart TB
  subgraph Pipeline_A["Pipeline A (Build)"]
    A1[Scan] --> A2[Filter] --> A3[Build Hash Table]
  end
  subgraph Pipeline_B["Pipeline B (Probe)"]
    B1[Rescan FT] --> B2[Probe Hash Table] --> B3[Produce]
  end
  Pipeline_A -->|dependency| Pipeline_B
```

This matters because:

* operators like hash-table build are “blocking” and create pipeline boundaries,
* and because graph joins/traversals can become very expensive if the engine can’t schedule and parallelize scans efficiently. ([Kuzu][7])

