## 11. Putting it together: why Kùzu behaves differently from SQL engines under graph workloads

### 11.1 Storage-level reason

Relational engines model relationships via joins across tables; Kùzu stores the relationship topology in **CSR join indices** (double indexed forward/back), so traversals and neighbor expansions can be executed with sequential adjacency scans rather than repeated index lookups. ([CIDRDB][2])

### 11.2 Execution-level reason

Kùzu’s vectorized primitives (`ValueVector`, selection state, chunks) plus factorization are designed to reduce copying and keep tight CPU-cache-friendly loops. ([Kuzu][6])

### 11.3 Plan-level reason

Kùzu’s ASP-Join uses “accumulate + semijoin filter + probe” to:

* preserve factorization structure,
* and drive build-side scans using SIP (scan only necessary properties/edges sequentially). ([CIDRDB][2])

